{"audio":{"src":"https://pub-13abeb101e2945d2a7f60234f6faf52e.r2.dev/materials/a2-modern-australia/audio/main-f833d18ae637.mp3","durationSec":341.472,"cues":[{"start":0,"end":2.62,"text":"Imagine your first week at a new office in Australia."},{"start":3.4,"end":5.06,"text":"Your manager says, \"Call me Chris.\""},{"start":5.9,"end":9,"text":"In the kitchen, someone you work with asks about your weekend."},{"start":9.98,"end":12.78,"text":"Many Australian workplaces are described as relaxed."},{"start":13.66,"end":17.8,"text":"People often use first names, friendly expressions and short forms."},{"start":18.76,"end":22.1,"text":"This style can help people feel that they are part of the same team."},{"start":22.78,"end":25.58,"text":"But a friendly style does not mean the work is unimportant."},{"start":26.42,"end":31.36,"text":"In our imagined office, a colleague later offers to help you finish a report on time."},{"start":31.36,"end":34.5,"text":"Friendly words make it easier to ask questions."},{"start":35.28,"end":40.52,"text":"The team still expects good work, clear plans and respect for other people's time."},{"start":41.4,"end":43.74,"text":"This is a broad picture, not a rule."},{"start":44.4,"end":48.16,"text":"A hospital, a bank and a building site can feel very different."},{"start":48.9,"end":51.06,"text":"Some people use a more formal style than others."},{"start":51.61,"end":55.5,"text":"A good first step is to listen and follow the people around you."},{"start":55.94,"end":58.94,"text":"Food in Australia today is a mix of many stories."},{"start":59.96,"end":65.04,"text":"Australia's first and oldest stories belong to Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples."},{"start":65.04,"end":70.96,"text":"Later, people came from many countries, and migration shaped food and celebrations."},{"start":72.06,"end":75.34,"text":"Today, restaurants and cafes serve food from many countries."},{"start":76.18,"end":79.76,"text":"For many people, food is a way to celebrate this mixed identity."},{"start":80.56,"end":82.64,"text":"Coffee is a short, clear example."},{"start":83.5,"end":88.22,"text":"After the Second World War, Greek and Italian migrants settled in Melbourne and Sydney."},{"start":89.04,"end":91.08,"text":"Their coffee habits became part of city life."},{"start":92,"end":96.8,"text":"Italian communities in regional Queensland also shaped how people drank coffee."},{"start":97.52,"end":100.6,"text":"So a daily cup of coffee can carry a migration story."},{"start":101.54,"end":103.96,"text":"The exact origin of the flat white is still unclear."},{"start":104.86,"end":106.8,"text":"Home food is often simple and shared."},{"start":107.44,"end":111.84,"text":"Friends and family gather for a barbecue in a backyard, in a park or at the beach."},{"start":112.3,"end":118.56,"text":"People may cook sausages, steak, chicken or prawns, and there are often plant-based options too."},{"start":119.28,"end":121.86,"text":"The meal is about time together, not only the food."},{"start":122.7,"end":124.98,"text":"Most of Australia's big cities are along the coast."},{"start":125.72,"end":130.4,"text":"Because of this, many people can go to the beach for a swim or to a park for lunch."},{"start":131.06,"end":139.48,"text":"On weekends, common social activities include brunch at a cafe, watching or playing sport, and walking in national parks near the city."},{"start":140.46,"end":143.92,"text":"Real examples show that outdoor life looks different in different places."},{"start":144.84,"end":147.86,"text":"In Sydney, some people enjoy swimming in the ocean at sunrise."},{"start":148.54,"end":151.36,"text":"In Canberra, people take weekend bike rides around the lake."},{"start":151.86,"end":154.66,"text":"In Perth, people enjoy picnics in Kings Park."},{"start":155.44,"end":157.48,"text":"So outdoor time is not only about surfing."},{"start":158.1,"end":161.62,"text":"It can mean a bike, a blanket on the grass or a quiet walk."},{"start":162.48,"end":163.46,"text":"There are also limits."},{"start":163.92,"end":167.16,"text":"These are common activities, not habits of every person."},{"start":167.16,"end":176.3,"text":"Holiday pictures show sunny, happy moments, but daily life also has work, travel time, family life and money to think about."},{"start":177.46,"end":181.42,"text":"Someone who works on Saturdays may enjoy the park on a weekday evening instead."},{"start":182.52,"end":185.6,"text":"Outdoor life is a chance for many people, not a rule for everyone."},{"start":186.72,"end":189.26,"text":"Many workers check messages on their phones at night."},{"start":189.76,"end":194.78,"text":"In Australia, there is now a clear rule about this called the right to disconnect."},{"start":195.82,"end":201.38,"text":"Employees can refuse to check, read or answer work messages outside their working hours."},{"start":202.2,"end":207.32,"text":"But there is an important exception: they may have to answer if refusing would be unreasonable."},{"start":208.62,"end":210.62,"text":"How do people decide what is unreasonable?"},{"start":211.96,"end":215.48,"text":"Several things matter:\n\nWhy did the employer make contact?"},{"start":216.18,"end":218.98,"text":"How much does it disturb the worker's free time?"},{"start":219.46,"end":221.88,"text":"Does the worker get extra pay for being available?"},{"start":222.68,"end":226.1,"text":"What are the worker's responsibilities and personal situation?"},{"start":227.1,"end":232.34,"text":"For example, workers who are on call or doing overtime may still have to respond."},{"start":232.84,"end":236.26,"text":"The rule does not stop a manager from sending a message after hours."},{"start":236.96,"end":240.12,"text":"It gives workers more power to say no in normal cases."},{"start":241.2,"end":245,"text":"Employers and employees are encouraged to talk about what they expect."},{"start":245,"end":250.42,"text":"A morning swim or a family barbecue needs time that work does not take."},{"start":251.32,"end":253.12,"text":"Protecting that time is not automatic."},{"start":253.52,"end":255.78,"text":"It still takes clear conversations and planning."},{"start":256.66,"end":258.88,"text":"Australians use the usual English words too."},{"start":260.06,"end":263.4,"text":"These informal choices are useful with friends and friendly colleagues."},{"start":264.1,"end":267.62,"text":"Usage varies, and some expressions are also heard outside Australia."},{"start":268.9,"end":270.48,"text":"Afternoon, arvo."},{"start":270.98,"end":273.54,"text":"A short, informal way to talk about the afternoon."},{"start":274.22,"end":275.16,"text":"“Are you free this arvo?”"},{"start":276.1,"end":278.14,"text":"Breakfast, brekkie."},{"start":278.5,"end":280.68,"text":"Use it in a casual conversation about food."},{"start":281.38,"end":282.72,"text":"“Let's have brekkie before work.”"},{"start":283.82,"end":285.58,"text":"Barbecue, barbie."},{"start":286.42,"end":288.5,"text":"This can mean the meal or the cooking equipment."},{"start":289.22,"end":290.62,"text":"“We're having a barbie on Sunday."},{"start":291.1,"end":292.04,"text":"Would you like to come?”"},{"start":293.12,"end":295.18,"text":"No problem, no worries."},{"start":295.9,"end":298.9,"text":"Use it when something is OK, or to reply to thanks."},{"start":299.54,"end":300.18,"text":"“Thanks for your help.”"},{"start":301.04,"end":301.56,"text":"“No worries!”"},{"start":301.56,"end":304.2,"text":"Friend / friendly address, mate."},{"start":304.92,"end":307.74,"text":"You can use it for a friend or when speaking in a friendly way."},{"start":308.24,"end":308.76,"text":"“Thanks, mate.”"},{"start":309.36,"end":311.04,"text":"It can be used with people of any gender."},{"start":311.76,"end":315.92,"text":"Listen to the situation first; it may feel too casual in a formal conversation."},{"start":317.76,"end":318.28,"text":"How are you?,"},{"start":318.74,"end":319.62,"text":"How are you going?"},{"start":320.26,"end":322.72,"text":"This asks how someone is, not how they travel."},{"start":323.32,"end":324.02,"text":"“How are you going?”"},{"start":324.62,"end":325.5,"text":"“Good, thanks."},{"start":326.1,"end":326.5,"text":"And you?”"},{"start":326.5,"end":328.94,"text":"Flip-flops, thongs."},{"start":329.58,"end":332.96,"text":"In Australia, these are light, open shoes for warm weather."},{"start":333.62,"end":335.24,"text":"“I've packed my thongs for the beach.”"},{"start":336.18,"end":341.04,"text":"The word can mean something different in other countries, so the situation helps you understand it."}],"words":[{"text":"Imagine","start":0,"end":0.36},{"text":"your","start":0.36,"end":0.62},{"text":"first","start":0.62,"end":0.86},{"text":"week","start":0.86,"end":1.16},{"text":"at","start":1.16,"end":1.42},{"text":"a","start":1.42,"end":1.54},{"text":"new","start":1.54,"end":1.68},{"text":"office","start":1.68,"end":2},{"text":"in","start":2,"end":2.18},{"text":"Australia","start":2.18,"end":2.62},{"text":"Your","start":3.4,"end":3.46},{"text":"manager","start":3.46,"end":3.76},{"text":"says","start":3.76,"end":4.2},{"text":"Call","start":4.66,"end":4.68},{"text":"me","start":4.68,"end":4.84},{"text":"Chris","start":4.84,"end":5.06},{"text":"In","start":5.9,"end":6.04},{"text":"the","start":6.04,"end":6.14},{"text":"kitchen","start":6.14,"end":6.5},{"text":"someone","start":6.96,"end":7.22},{"text":"you","start":7.22,"end":7.4},{"text":"work","start":7.4,"end":7.58},{"text":"with","start":7.58,"end":7.82},{"text":"asks","start":7.82,"end":8.18},{"text":"about","start":8.18,"end":8.48},{"text":"your","start":8.48,"end":8.66},{"text":"weekend","start":8.66,"end":9},{"text":"Many","start":9.98,"end":10.18},{"text":"Australian","start":10.18,"end":10.6},{"text":"workplaces","start":10.6,"end":11.14},{"text":"are","start":11.14,"end":11.58},{"text":"described","start":11.58,"end":12},{"text":"as","start":12,"end":12.24},{"text":"relaxed","start":12.24,"end":12.78},{"t